# The two Noir wasm modules the Noir Studio web bundle fetches at run time.
#
# WHY A PIN FILE AND NOT `flake.nix`'s `noir` INPUT
#
# `flake.nix` already pins a `noir` (ref `codetracer-temp`, rev 5e98f904) and
# that pin is NOT this one. It supplies the NATIVE `nargo` the recorder builds
# against; these are `wasm32-unknown-unknown` builds of two different crates
# from the `codetracer` branch, consumed by a browser and by nothing else.
# Sharing one pin between them would couple a desktop toolchain bump to a web
# deploy in both directions, which is worse than two pins that each say what
# they are for.
#
# WHY PINNED AT ALL, rather than tracking the branch head. A deploy that
# rebuilds from a moving ref cannot be reproduced, and the provenance string
# the entry document carries — `noir@codetracer <rev> compiler/wasm` — would
# name a commit that had already moved. The revision below is what the page
# tells a user it is running.
#
# TO BUMP: change NOIR_REV, run `bash ci/deploy/build-noir-wasm.sh <out-dir>`,
# and check the two sizes in the log against the ones recorded here.

NOIR_REPO=https://github.com/metacraft-labs/noir
NOIR_REF=codetracer
# WHAT THIS BUMP BUYS: a contract can be compiled in a DEBUGGABLE form.
#
# At 6c590c77 the mode dispatcher derived `as_contract = mode == "contract"` and
# `for_debugging = mode == "debug"` — two booleans off one string — while
# `vfs::compile_resolved` takes them independently. `as_contract && for_debugging`
# was unreachable, and since `debug` is `compile_main` and a contract crate has
# no `main`, an Aztec contract could not be made steppable in one compile or in
# ten. The revision below adds the `contract-debug` mode that sets both, and
# refuses an unknown mode instead of degrading it to a `program` compile.
#
# PUSHED, AND THAT WAS THE GATE. `build-noir-wasm.sh` CLONES this rev; it does
# not read a checkout. So while the revision existed only in a local noir
# worktree this pin named a commit no builder could resolve, and no deploy
# could have succeeded. It is now on `browser/contract-debug-mode` at
# metacraft-labs/noir, verified not by the push reporting success but by
# fetching the bare rev into an EMPTY clone that had never seen this machine:
#
#   git init && git remote add origin https://github.com/metacraft-labs/noir
#   git fetch --depth=1 origin 9d4e40a64297101aede7bef453acaa5ea0586744
#
# resolves, and `compiler/wasm/src/compile_vfs.rs` at the fetched commit
# carries `contract-debug`. That is the same operation the build script
# performs, so what was checked is what the builder does.
NOIR_REV=9d4e40a64297101aede7bef453acaa5ea0586744

# The noir workspace resolves `codetracer_trace_types` and
# `codetracer_trace_writer_nim` through PATH dependencies on a sibling checkout
# (`../codetracer-trace-format`). Without it `cargo build` fails before
# compiling anything, with an error naming a missing Cargo.toml rather than a
# missing repository — so the sibling is pinned here too.
TRACE_FORMAT_REPO=https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer-trace-format
TRACE_FORMAT_REV=235e377ec66b7d140e9c75828e805d53945824e3

# Measured at the revisions above (2026-09-01, aarch64-darwin). The compiler
# moved 15862494 -> 15858329 (-4165) across the contract-debug change; the
# tracer was not touched and its figure is carried forward unmeasured, which is
# what "advisory" below licenses. Both builds of the new revision from the same
# directory gave the identical 15858329.
#
# ADVISORY, AND THE BUILD SCRIPT ONLY REPORTS ON THEM. These are not
# reproducible byte-for-byte and that was measured, not assumed: building the
# same revision twice from two different checkout DIRECTORIES gave 15862494 vs
# 15862541 and 4618464 vs 4619415. rustc embeds source paths, so the length of
# the build directory reaches the artifact. A rustc bump moves them further.
#
# So an exact-match assertion here would fail every deploy for reasons that
# have nothing to do with the product, and the first person to meet that would
# delete the check. What it is good for is ORDER OF MAGNITUDE: a module that
# arrives at 4 KB is a build that emitted an error page, and that shows up
# against these numbers instantly.
#
# The check that actually protects a visitor is elsewhere and is exact:
# `ci/test/web_deploy_guard.nim` compares the size the entry document DECLARES
# against the file in the publish directory, and both come from the same
# assembly run — so it catches a stale document over fresh bytes, which is the
# failure that reached production in the sibling campaign.
EXPECT_COMPILER_BYTES=15858329
EXPECT_TRACER_BYTES=4618464
